 - **IR (OV7251).** Two separate problems as well. Its power-enable GPIO is registered as `avdd`, but `ov7251` requests `vdda`, so the sensor's first I2C write fails with `-121` and it never probes at all. Once it does probe, the IR illuminator hangs off the sensor's strobe output, and mainline's `ov7251` init programs that strobe with pattern `0xa5` (`10100101`), so the LED fires on alternate frames and half the frames come out dark; Windows programs `0xff`. #2252 fixes both.
 - **Rear autofocus.** On 6.19 the `dw9719` VCM driver no longer binds ([#2225](https://github.com/linux-surface/linux-surface/issues/2225); fixed upstream, backport in [#2123](https://github.com/linux-surface/linux-surface/pull/2123)). On the Pro 7+ this only loses focus control; the cameras still enumerate.
 
+#### Surface Laptop 1 cameras
+
+The original Surface Laptop has two front sensors on Intel IPU3. Both have delivered images on a physical SL1, but the complete stack is not included in the tested Fedora 45 linux-surface packages.
+
+* **Color (OV9734):** the kernel needs a CSI endpoint and working clock/reset sequencing. The SL1 test used the [7.3 patches in #2401](https://github.com/linux-surface/linux-surface/issues/2401), including deferred probe when the endpoint is not ready. [Kernel PR #186](https://github.com/linux-surface/kernel/pull/186) proposes a related implementation. Userspace also needs the [libcamera OV9734 IPU3 changes](https://patchwork.libcamera.org/cover/28869/). The public series, adapted to current libcamera, delivered 1280×704. A further single-stream change delivered native 1280×720 at 30 fps with a 33.3 ms frame-duration request. A separately tuned stack also delivered 720p in GNOME Camera.
+* **Infrared (OV7251):** raw monochrome capture and the IR illuminator are separate features. Desktop apps need a source that can unpack the IPU3 grayscale format. The SL1 test used a PipeWire bridge and a backport of the [exposure-synchronized strobe patch](https://lore.kernel.org/linux-media/20261113024059.1087031-9-antoine.pichot@gmail.com/). It delivered 640×480 at 30 fps in ambient light, or 15 illuminated frames per second because the sensor pulses on alternate exposures. The emitter switched off when capture stopped, including after a client crash. This test did not cover face authentication.
+
+The [tested patch versions and capture results](https://github.com/Dvaderfun/linux-surface/tree/sl1-camera-reference/experiments/sl1-camera) are available for comparison. The tested CIO2 driver streams one sensor at a time. Close the other camera client before switching. To diagnose color, check for the OV9734 with `cam -l`, then capture frames with `cam -c '<ID from cam -l>' -s role=viewfinder,width=640,height=480 --capture=30`. To check what desktop applications can select, look under **Video → Sources** in `wpctl status`. An OV7251 entry in `media-ctl -p` by itself does not establish desktop or illuminator support.
+
 ### IPU3 Firmware
 
 The IPU3 used by the Surface requires some firmware to support the devices which is loaded at runtime. 
